    "category": "Calculator Guides",
    "content": "# Free Online TDEE Calculator: What to Check Before Trusting a Result\n\nA free online TDEE calculator can be a convenient way to estimate daily energy expenditure. But a polished interface does not automatically make a result accurate. Before using any calculator, check what it measures, which equation it uses and what assumptions sit behind the final number.\n\nThe [ReelHooks TDEE calculator](/tdee-calculator) is designed to show an estimated BMR, TDEE and an illustrative goal-based calorie figure. Here is a practical checklist you can use with any online tool.\n\n## 1. Does it explain its equation?\n\nA useful calculator should identify the method behind its estimate. The Mifflin–St Jeor equation is one commonly used prediction equation for resting energy expenditure. It was published in 1990 after measurements in 498 healthy adults. The original research is available through [PubMed](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2305711/).\n\nAn equation is still a prediction. It does not directly measure your metabolism and may be less suitable for people outside the population in which it was studied.\n\n## 2. Are the units clear?\n\nLook for a clear metric and imperial option. Weight may be entered in kilograms or pounds, while height may be entered in centimetres or feet and inches. A unit mistake can change the output substantially.\n\n## 3. Are activity levels explained?\n\nA calculator should describe its activity categories rather than presenting unexplained labels. Activity multipliers are rough categories and can be difficult to choose when a person's routine varies.\n\n## 4. Does it explain limitations?\n\nBe cautious of websites promising a perfectly accurate number, guaranteed weight change or a medically personalised plan from only a few inputs. A general calculator cannot assess medical history, medications, pregnancy, breastfeeding or an eating-disorder history.\n\n## 5. Does it respect privacy?\n\nCheck whether the website explains what happens to information entered into the form. Avoid entering sensitive health information into tools without a clear privacy policy.\n\n## A sensible way to compare calculators\n\nCompare the equation, units, activity factors, explanations and privacy practices. Do not simply choose the highest or lowest result. If two calculators differ, their assumptions may differ too.\n\nA free tool can be useful for learning, provided you understand that it is an estimate. For personal nutrition decisions, consult a qualified professional.",
