    "content": "# How to Use an Online TDEE Calculator Correctly\n\nAn online TDEE calculator is only as useful as the information entered into it. Incorrect units, an unsuitable activity category or a misunderstood result can make a reasonable equation look unreliable.\n\nThis guide explains how to use the [ReelHooks online TDEE calculator](/tdee-calculator) and how to interpret the estimate.\n\n## Step 1: Choose the correct unit system\n\nSelect metric if you know your weight in kilograms and height in centimetres. Select imperial if your measurements are in pounds, feet and inches. Do not convert one field and forget another.\n\n## Step 2: Enter the requested details carefully\n\nEnter your age and current measurements accurately. The calculator uses sex-specific versions of the Mifflin–St Jeor equation. These are statistical assumptions used by the equation, not a complete description of an individual's biology.\n\n## Step 3: Choose activity based on your usual routine\n\nActivity multipliers are broad categories. Consider your overall daily movement, including work, walking and exercise. Do not automatically choose the highest category because you completed one hard workout.\n\n## Step 4: Understand the result labels\n\nBMR is an estimate of energy used at rest. TDEE is the estimated daily total after applying an activity multiplier. A maintenance estimate is not an exact requirement for each day.\n\nSome calculators also show goal-based figures. Treat these as mathematical illustrations, not personalised prescriptions. A fixed deficit or surplus may not be appropriate for every person.\n\n## Step 5: Know when the tool is not suitable\n\nGeneral adult calculators should not be used to set calorie targets for children or teenagers, pregnancy, breastfeeding, eating-disorder recovery or medical nutrition therapy. A healthcare professional can assess individual circumstances.\n\n## Why two online calculators may disagree\n\nDifferent tools may use different equations, activity factors, rounding rules or input ranges. Their outputs can therefore vary even when the same measurements are entered. Read the method before comparing the numbers.\n\nUse an online calculator as an educational starting point. If the result will influence a health decision, discuss it with a registered dietitian or qualified healthcare professional.",
