    "category": "India Fitness Guides",
    "content": "# TDEE Calculator India: Using Metric Units and Understanding Your Estimate\n\nIf you are searching for a TDEE calculator in India, the first thing to check is whether the tool supports metric measurements. Many people record weight in kilograms and height in centimetres, so a calculator with a clear metric option can make data entry easier.\n\nUse the [ReelHooks TDEE calculator](/tdee-calculator) and select Metric (kg, cm). Enter your measurements carefully and choose an activity level that reflects your usual routine.\n\n## What does the result mean?\n\nTDEE means total daily energy expenditure. It estimates energy used at rest, through movement and exercise, and during digestion. The result is an estimate, not a measurement of metabolism and not a personal medical recommendation.\n\nThe calculator estimates resting energy expenditure using the Mifflin–St Jeor equation, then applies an activity multiplier. The original equation was developed from a study of healthy adults; its use in a web calculator does not make it a clinical assessment.\n\n## Think about your whole day when selecting activity\n\nActivity is more than gym sessions. A person may walk to public transport, work on their feet, do household tasks or spend most of the day seated. Another person may train regularly but otherwise have a sedentary routine. Choose the category that most closely describes your overall week.\n\nThe activity labels are broad. If your schedule changes from day to day, a single multiplier will not capture every difference.\n\n## Food culture is personal\n\nThere is no single Indian diet. Meals differ by region, household, religion, budget, preference and availability. Rice, roti, dal, vegetables, dairy, eggs, fish, meat and many other foods can all appear in different eating patterns. A calorie estimate does not tell you which foods you should eat.\n\nIf you track food, use portion information that matches how you actually prepare and serve meals. Restaurant portions, homemade recipes and cooking oils can vary. Avoid assuming that one standard serving applies to every household.\n\n## Important limitations\n\nA general adult calculator should not be used to prescribe calorie targets for teenagers, pregnancy, breastfeeding or medical conditions. If you have diabetes, kidney disease, digestive conditions, take medication affecting appetite or weight, or have a history of disordered eating, seek individual advice.\n\nThe calculator can help explain energy expenditure. It cannot replace a registered dietitian or healthcare professional who understands your circumstances.",
