    "category": "India Fitness Guides",
    "content": "# Choosing an Activity Level for a TDEE Calculator in India\n\nOne of the most confusing parts of a TDEE calculator is the activity dropdown. The labels can look simple, but real routines are not. A person's day may include desk work, commuting, household responsibilities, walking and a few exercise sessions. No single category can describe every detail.\n\nWhen using the [ReelHooks TDEE calculator](/tdee-calculator), choose the activity option that best reflects your usual week rather than your most active day.\n\n## What activity multipliers are trying to represent\n\nA TDEE calculator first estimates resting energy expenditure. It then multiplies that estimate by an activity factor to approximate total daily energy use. The factor is a shortcut. It is not a direct measurement of your movement or a personalised activity score.\n\n## Look beyond formal workouts\n\nConsider these parts of your routine:\n\n- **Work:** Do you sit for most of the day, stand regularly or perform physical tasks?\n- **Commuting:** Do you walk, cycle, use public transport or travel mainly by car or two-wheeler?\n- **Daily movement:** Do you take regular walks or spend long periods sitting?\n- **Exercise:** How often and how intensely do you train?\n- **Recovery:** Does your activity vary because of rest days, illness or changing shifts?\n\nThese examples are prompts for thinking, not a formula for assigning a precise multiplier.\n\n## Why the highest category is not always best\n\nA few demanding workouts do not necessarily make the entire week extra active. Conversely, someone with a physically demanding job may use substantial energy outside formal exercise. Focus on the overall pattern.\n\n## What if your routine changes?\n\nIf you have a different schedule on weekdays and weekends, use an average description of your normal week. If your job or training routine changes for a sustained period, you may want to recalculate the estimate.\n\n## Do not treat the output as a prescription\n\nAn activity multiplier cannot account for every medical or personal factor. General calculators are not designed to prescribe nutrition for minors, pregnancy, breastfeeding or medical nutrition therapy. Seek individual guidance where appropriate.\n\nThe aim is not to find a perfect label. It is to understand that activity selection is an assumption, and that assumption affects the estimate.",
