    "category": "Science & Methods",
    "content": "# TDEE Calculator Formula Explained: Mifflin–St Jeor Step by Step\n\nA TDEE calculator does not directly measure how much energy your body uses. It estimates resting energy expenditure with a prediction equation and then applies an activity multiplier. Understanding those two steps makes the final number easier to interpret.\n\nThe [ReelHooks TDEE calculator](/tdee-calculator) uses the Mifflin–St Jeor method. The original study was published in the *American Journal of Clinical Nutrition* in 1990 and included 498 healthy adults. The paper is indexed on [PubMed](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2305711/).\n\n## The Mifflin–St Jeor equations\n\nFor the commonly used sex-specific forms:\n\n**Men:** REE = (10 × weight in kg) + (6.25 × height in cm) − (5 × age in years) + 5\n\n**Women:** REE = (10 × weight in kg) + (6.25 × height in cm) − (5 × age in years) − 161\n\nREE means resting energy expenditure. Many consumer calculators label this estimate BMR, although basal and resting measurements are not perfectly interchangeable in every technical context.\n\n## From resting estimate to TDEE\n\nThe next step is:\n\n**Estimated TDEE = estimated resting energy expenditure × activity factor**\n\nActivity factors are broad categories such as sedentary, lightly active or moderately active. They are assumptions, not a direct record of steps, occupation or exercise.\n\n## A simplified example\n\nImagine an adult enters a weight of 70 kg, height of 170 cm and age of 30. The calculator first applies the relevant equation to estimate resting energy expenditure. It then multiplies the result by the selected activity factor. Changing the activity selection changes the estimated TDEE even though the person's measurements stay the same.\n\nThis example explains the method, not a recommended calorie intake for any individual.\n\n## What are the formula's limitations?\n\nThe equation predicts an average from a study population. It cannot fully account for body composition, individual physiology, health conditions, medication, pregnancy, breastfeeding or unusual activity patterns. It may be less suitable for people outside the studied adult population.\n\nA formula is useful because it makes an estimate consistent and explainable. It is not a substitute for indirect calorimetry or professional assessment when precision matters.",
