    "category": "Science & Methods",
    "content": "# BMR vs TDEE: Formula, Differences and a Worked Explanation\n\nBMR and TDEE are related, but they do not mean the same thing. BMR refers to the energy needed to support essential functions at rest under strict measurement conditions. TDEE estimates the energy used across a full day, including movement and digestion.\n\nA [BMR and TDEE calculator](/tdee-calculator) can show both estimates, but it is important to understand what each number represents.\n\n## What does BMR estimate?\n\nBasal metabolic rate is the energy your body needs for basic functions such as breathing, circulation and maintaining body temperature. Consumer calculators often estimate this using a prediction equation rather than measuring it in a laboratory.\n\nThe Mifflin–St Jeor equation is a commonly used method for estimating resting energy expenditure from age, height, weight and a sex variable. The original research is available through [PubMed](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2305711/).\n\n## What does TDEE estimate?\n\nTDEE adds the energy used through daily movement, planned exercise and digestion. A simplified calculator estimates it by multiplying resting energy expenditure by an activity factor.\n\nThat means TDEE changes when the selected activity category changes. The activity factor is broad and cannot precisely describe every person's day.\n\n## Why the distinction matters\n\nBMR is not a recommended daily calorie target. It is an estimate of resting energy use. TDEE is often used as a rough estimate of average daily energy expenditure, but it is still not a personalised prescription.\n\nIf two calculators show different BMR or TDEE results, compare their equations, activity multipliers, units and rounding. Different assumptions can produce different estimates.\n\n## When to seek individual advice\n\nGeneral equations are not designed to set calorie targets for children and teenagers, pregnancy, breastfeeding, eating-disorder recovery or medical nutrition therapy. A registered dietitian or healthcare professional can consider information a simple online form does not collect.\n\nUse the distinction to understand the numbers, not to turn them into strict rules.",
