    "category": "Weight Management Education",
    "content": "# Why Your Weight Changes Even When Your TDEE Estimate Stays the Same\n\nA TDEE calculator gives an estimate based on inputs and an activity assumption. Your scale, however, reflects much more than body fat. This is why daily weight can move even when your routine appears unchanged.\n\nIf you have used the [TDEE calculator](/tdee-calculator), remember that the output is an estimate of average energy expenditure, not a prediction of tomorrow morning's scale reading.\n\n## Common reasons for short-term changes\n\n- **Fluid balance:** hydration and changes in sodium intake can affect water weight.\n- **Carbohydrate intake:** stored glycogen is associated with water, so changes in carbohydrate intake can influence scale readings.\n- **Digestion:** food and fluid still moving through the digestive system affect total body weight.\n- **Exercise:** unfamiliar or strenuous activity can temporarily affect fluid balance.\n- **Hormonal variation:** menstrual-cycle changes can influence water retention for some people.\n\nThese changes do not necessarily indicate a change in body fat.\n\n## A calculator is not a scale predictor\n\nTDEE equations estimate energy expenditure from population averages. They do not know your exact movement, body composition, health status or daily energy use. Two people with identical calculator inputs may have different actual needs.\n\nA single weigh-in cannot prove that the equation is right or wrong. If monitoring weight is appropriate for you, consider longer-term patterns and use consistent conditions. If weighing causes anxiety or disordered behaviours, avoid it and seek support.\n\n## Avoid reacting to one number\n\nDo not make drastic food or exercise changes because of a single reading. If you are concerned about weight changes, appetite, fatigue or other symptoms, speak with a healthcare professional.\n\nGeneral calculators are not intended for children, teenagers, pregnancy, breastfeeding or medical nutrition therapy. The safest interpretation is to treat TDEE as an educational estimate and not a strict daily rule.",
