    "excerpt": "A practical checklist for choosing an online TDEE calculator, from transparent formulas and metric units to privacy and clear health warnings.",
    "category": "Calculator Reviews & Methods",
    "content": "# Best Online TDEE Calculator: 7 Features Worth Comparing\n\nThe best online TDEE calculator is not necessarily the one with the boldest claim or the most detailed dashboard. A useful tool should be transparent, easy to operate and honest about what a formula can estimate.\n\nUse this checklist when comparing tools, including the [ReelHooks TDEE calculator](/tdee-calculator).\n\n## 1. A named equation\n\nLook for an explanation of the prediction method. The Mifflin–St Jeor equation is one commonly used option. The original study is available through [PubMed](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2305711/).\n\n## 2. Clear metric and imperial inputs\n\nThe tool should make it obvious whether weight is in kilograms or pounds and whether height is in centimetres or feet and inches.\n\n## 3. Explained activity levels\n\nActivity categories should include descriptions. A multiplier is an approximation of an overall routine, not a precise movement measurement.\n\n## 4. Separate BMR and TDEE results\n\nA clear interface distinguishes resting energy expenditure from estimated total daily energy expenditure. It should not label BMR as a daily calorie target.\n\n## 5. Honest goal settings\n\nIf the tool displays weight-loss or weight-gain figures, it should explain that these are arithmetic illustrations rather than personalised medical advice.\n\n## 6. Privacy information\n\nA calculator should make it clear how information entered into the page is handled. Avoid sharing sensitive health details with websites that do not explain their privacy practices.\n\n## 7. Accessible design and useful explanations\n\nInputs should work on mobile, labels should be clear and results should be understandable without specialist knowledge. A good tool also explains its limitations.\n\n## A note about accuracy\n\nNo general online calculator is guaranteed to be exact. Prediction equations can differ from measured energy expenditure for an individual. If precision matters for clinical care, a qualified professional can advise on appropriate assessment.\n\nChoose the calculator that explains its method, not the one that promises the most.",
