    "category": "Calculator Guides",
    "content": "# Looking for an NHS TDEE Calculator? Understand the Difference Between Tools\n\nPeople searching for an NHS TDEE calculator may be trying to find a trusted way to understand daily energy needs. It is important to distinguish an independent TDEE calculator from information or tools published by the UK's National Health Service.\n\nReelHooks is an independent website. Its [TDEE calculator](/tdee-calculator) is not an NHS tool, is not endorsed by the NHS and should not be presented as an official UK health service.\n\n## What does a TDEE calculator estimate?\n\nTDEE means total daily energy expenditure. A general calculator estimates resting energy expenditure and applies an activity factor to approximate daily energy use. It is a mathematical estimate, not a clinical assessment.\n\nThe ReelHooks tool uses the Mifflin–St Jeor equation, a prediction method published in 1990. The original study is indexed on [PubMed](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2305711/).\n\n## Why a public health resource and a TDEE calculator are different\n\nA calculator gives an estimated number based on a limited set of inputs. Public health guidance may consider broader issues such as health conditions, age, medication, pregnancy and individual circumstances. These serve different purposes.\n\nIf you are looking for official NHS advice, visit the [NHS website](https://www.nhs.uk/) directly and check the source and date of any guidance. Do not assume that a third-party calculator represents NHS recommendations.\n\n## When a general calculator is not enough\n\nA basic TDEE tool is not designed to prescribe calorie intake for children or teenagers, pregnancy, breastfeeding, eating-disorder recovery or medical nutrition therapy. If you have a health concern or need a personal plan, speak with a qualified healthcare professional or registered dietitian.\n\n## How to assess a third-party calculator\n\nCheck whether the equation is named, units are clear, activity levels are explained and limitations are visible. Be cautious of any website claiming official status without evidence or promising exact results.\n\nThe purpose of this guide is to clarify the difference between an independent estimate and official health information, not to replace NHS advice.",
