    "content": "# FITTR and TDEE Calculators: Questions to Ask Before Comparing Results\n\nIf you are comparing a fitness app such as FITTR with a standalone TDEE calculator, start by checking what each tool actually calculates. Different products may use different equations, activity assumptions and input methods, so their results may not match.\n\nReelHooks is independent and is not affiliated with FITTR. This article does not claim to have tested or verified FITTR's current calculator or app features. It offers a neutral method for comparing estimates.\n\n## Compare the method first\n\nCheck whether each tool identifies its resting-energy equation. The ReelHooks [TDEE calculator](/tdee-calculator) uses Mifflin–St Jeor, followed by an activity multiplier. The original research is indexed on [PubMed](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2305711/).\n\nIf another tool uses a different method, the output may differ even when you enter the same measurements.\n\n## Check the activity assumptions\n\nOne tool may ask for exercise frequency while another asks for an overall activity category. Those inputs are not necessarily equivalent. Review the exact wording and avoid assuming that the same label means the same multiplier.\n\n## Compare like with like\n\nFor a fair comparison, use the same age, height, weight, unit system and relevant equation settings. Check whether one result is BMR, resting energy expenditure, maintenance calories or a goal-based target. These terms should not be treated as interchangeable.\n\n## Look at privacy and transparency\n\nReview each service's current privacy policy and terms before entering personal information. Check whether the tool explains how data is stored or used. Product features and policies can change, so consult the provider's own current documentation.\n\n## What if the numbers still differ?\n\nThat does not automatically mean one tool is broken. Prediction equations are estimates, and activity factors introduce additional uncertainty. If a number will guide a health decision, ask a registered dietitian or healthcare professional for individual advice.\n\nUse comparisons to understand assumptions, not to find the most convenient number.",
