    "id": "tdee-teens-safety-guide",
    "title": "TDEE Calculators for Teenagers: Why Adult Calorie Estimates Need Caution",
    "slug": "tdee-calculator-for-teens-safety-and-limitations",
    "excerpt": "A safety-first explanation of why adult TDEE calculators are not appropriate for setting calorie targets during adolescence and where to seek support.",
    "category": "Health & Safety",
    "content": "# TDEE Calculators for Teenagers: Why Adult Calorie Estimates Need Caution\n\nTeenagers and parents may search for a TDEE calculator to understand energy needs. However, a general adult calculator should not be used to set calorie targets for someone who is still growing.\n\nAdolescence involves growth and development. Energy and nutrient needs can change with age, development, activity and individual health. A basic form asking for age, height, weight and activity cannot assess those needs adequately.\n\n## Why an adult equation is not enough\n\nThe Mifflin–St Jeor equation used by many adult calculators was developed from research in healthy adults aged 19 to 78. It was not designed as a personal nutrition prescription for younger teenagers. The original study is indexed on [PubMed](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2305711/).\n\nA calculator may technically accept a teenage age, but that does not mean the result is validated or suitable for that person.\n\n## What should families do instead?\n\nIf a young person has questions about growth, sports nutrition, appetite or weight, speak with a paediatrician, family doctor or registered dietitian with experience in adolescent nutrition. They can consider growth patterns, development, health and the young person's circumstances.\n\nAvoid restrictive calorie targets, public weight comparisons or pressure to change body size based on an online result. Support regular meals, a positive relationship with food and appropriate activity without turning a calculator output into a rule.\n\n## A note about this website's calculator\n\nThe ReelHooks TDEE calculator is a general educational tool for adults. It is not intended to calculate calorie targets for teenagers. Do not use its output to plan a young person's diet.\n\nThis article is general information and is not a substitute for clinical assessment. If you are concerned about a teenager's eating, growth or wellbeing, seek professional support rather than relying on an online calculator.",
